`check` runs the same pipeline `build` does — parse, link, compose,
codegen — and then throws the document away. It is the CI gate: exit 0
means every entry point it found compiles.

A file that compiles prints nothing at all. There is no document to
show and no path to report, and silence is what a gate that passed
should look like, so the whole result is the exit code.

```console
$ hllc check web.hll

```

A file that does not compile prints exactly the diagnostic `build`
would, on stderr, and exits non-zero. The two modes share one pipeline,
so a message can't drift between the run that gates a change and the
run that ships it.

```console
$ hllc check broken.hll
? 1
broken.hll:3:8: unknown template `hardened`

```

`check` accepts a directory in both shapes `build` does, since a gate
wants the whole tree in one command. Flat mode needs no `--out` here:
`build` requires one because it has however many files' worth of output
to place, and `check` places none.

```console
$ hllc check services

```

Co-located mode is the same walk `build` makes, and matters more here:
`build` with no `--out` writes each service directory's document *in
place*, so `check` over the same tree has to leave every one of those
directories untouched. `build_tests.rs` is what asserts that nothing
appeared, since a directory comparison can only speak about files it
names.

```console
$ hllc check homelab

```

A directory with no entry point in it says so, the way `build` does —
a run that quietly does nothing is easy to mistake for one that
passed — with the verb it was actually asked for rather than "built".

```console
$ hllc check library
library: none of the .hll files here declare a service — nothing was checked

```
